WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.620
What’s up guys?

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.280
Jared again.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.900
Perhaps no word describes the humor of Rick
and Morty more concisely/precisely than "cynical."

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.160
"What people call love is just a chemical
reaction that compels animals to breed".

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.730
The show has a reputation for taking the piss
out of anything one might consider “sacred,”

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:26.060
whether it’s religion, existence, individuality,
or family.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:30.699
In particular, the idea of marital love has
been taken down a few pegs, primarily through

00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:32.620
Beth and Jerry’s dysfunctional marriage.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:37.720
But with them reuniting at the end of season
3, can we expect a shift in the way the show

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.290
depicts love?

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.820
Episode 2 of season 4 was released last weekend
and although it’s full of the same Rick

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.910
and Morty snark we’ve come to eagerly expect,
"Dad, I wanna say something.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:50.761
I started today disgusted and embarrassed
to be your son, and then later I though we

00:00:50.761 --> 00:00:54.070
were gonna die because you're a loser."

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:55.870
"There's no more is there?"

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.740
"Nope, now quit f***ing up and let's go."

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:02.580
It also frames love in a way that makes the
show seem a lot more wholesome and positive

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.250
than it possibly ever has.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:08.450
So let’s find out if nihilism is getting
old in this quicktake on Rick and Morty Season

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:26.090
4 Episode 2: The Old Man and

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:53.360
the Seat.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:11.579
And

00:02:11.579 --> 00:02:19.299
of course, spoilers ahead.

00:02:19.299 --> 00:02:28.209
Okay, let’s dive into a quick recap: Rick
is on the hunt for the man who used his secret

00:02:28.209 --> 00:02:33.209
pastoral toilet, while his monogatron intern
Glutey convinces Jerry to help him develop

00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:38.489
an app called LoveFinderrz, which precisely
connects people with soulmate after soulmate

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:39.769
after soulmate.

00:02:39.769 --> 00:02:44.489
Beth tries to stop Summer from engaging in
app-fueled serial monogamy while Jerry and

00:02:44.489 --> 00:02:48.500
Morty travel to the monogatron mothership
to try and shut the app down.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:53.529
The toilet trespasser turns out to be a widower
named Tony, who sees a kindred spirit in Rick,

00:02:53.529 --> 00:02:56.980
as they both need to shy away from the world
during poop time.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:00.079
Tony tries to strike up a friendship, but
Rick isn’t down.

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:04.580
Meanwhile, Beth finally corners Summer and
tries to convince her to stop her dating bender.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:09.560
Luckily, Jerry has convinced Glutey to sabotage
the app by setting up an adwall, forcing Summer

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.730
to delete it.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:15.529
After learning that Tony died, Rick returns
to his toilet and solemnly reflects on his

00:03:15.529 --> 00:03:16.709
loneliness.

00:03:16.709 --> 00:03:17.709
The end.

00:03:17.709 --> 00:03:20.450
For the purposes of this discussion on love,
we’re going to be focusing on the B-plot:

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:24.590
Summer’s addiction to the dating app, and
Beth’s mission to stop her.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:28.840
The monogatrons are able to so thoroughly
distract humans because, when faced with an

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:33.900
abundance of partners, they’ll obsessively
blow through relationship after relationship.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:39.799
“As a pre-app civilization, your love’s
value is defined by scarcity.

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:45.999
You are highly trained to seek it and you
have no idea how to maintain it.

00:03:45.999 --> 00:03:51.559
Had you bothered to master love, you would
have learned that its as abundant as water

00:03:51.559 --> 00:03:52.989
you know what isn’t?

00:03:52.989 --> 00:03:55.769
Water, that s*** runs out”

00:03:55.769 --> 00:04:01.069
Because humanity views love as a finite resource,
and our number of potential partners as limited,

00:04:01.069 --> 00:04:05.930
earth is easily “disrupted” when the monogatrons
introduce an app that connects each person

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.029
to a soulmate with terrifying efficiency.

00:04:09.029 --> 00:04:14.249
People don’t just pair up, they leap from
partner to partner like alien frogs on a lillypad.

00:04:14.249 --> 00:04:20.620
Summer goes from Danny, to Jeff- to this devastatingly
unhygienic girl- to this dude in no time.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.300
“Stupid phones are destroying us!”

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:27.210
It’s a pretty clear condemnation of modern
dating apps like tinder, OKCupid, Bumble,

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:32.240
Grindr, etc, which inundate users with so
many choices that there’s little incentive

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.639
to invest in any one relationship.

00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:40.319
By constantly offering another newer, shinier
mate, these apps often either encourage promiscuity

00:04:40.319 --> 00:04:43.750
or turn people into serial 3-hour monogamists.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:48.129
Compare the modern day Tindr hunt for love
with the way earlier generations found romantic

00:04:48.129 --> 00:04:49.129
partners.

00:04:49.129 --> 00:04:52.509
Back in agrarian times, you pretty much had
the choice of people within your small town,

00:04:52.509 --> 00:04:56.620
so if Annie or Timmy from the farm next door
wasn’t your type you kind of just had to

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:57.620
deal.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:02.669
Love literally WAS a scarce resource in a
pre-industrial, and pre-internet world - your

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:04.960
pool of options was just smaller.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.789
But people still found lifelong companionship.

00:05:07.789 --> 00:05:12.220
Today, you could reject the population of
a small agrarian community in the span of

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:13.370
60 seconds.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:17.800
So if a “pre-app” society’s vision of
love is defined by scarcity, we clearly see

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:21.770
that introducing an infinite number of options
is NOT the answer.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:26.490
But how could an abundance of choice negatively
affect one’s ability to find love?

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:29.830
We all want to find the BEST partner, right?

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:33.349
To answer that, we’re looking to French
philosopher Alain Badiou, and specifically

00:05:33.349 --> 00:05:35.669
his book “In Praise of Love.”

00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:40.100
Badiou claims that online dating leads to
a risk free version of love that erases the

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.280
possibility of encountering difference.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:47.729
Basically, when you have the ability to algorithmically
pick a potential mate by their political preferences,

00:05:47.729 --> 00:05:53.490
favorite movies, height, hair color, socioeconomic
background, etc, you filter out the possibility

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:57.900
of organically encountering something you
don’t know you want- it doesn’t leave

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.120
room for that potential meetcute.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:03.300
We see this with the hyper-efficiency of LoveFinderzz.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:08.319
The app scans your face and immediately pairs
you with a partner that fits your every desire.

00:06:08.319 --> 00:06:11.310
But as we see, this love doesn’t last.

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:16.759
People don’t have just one compatible partner,
and desire, like most things, changes, and

00:06:16.759 --> 00:06:19.120
people move on to chase the next desire.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:22.199
So if not personal fulfillment, what is love?

00:06:22.199 --> 00:06:26.469
"Baby don't hurt me, don't hurt me, no more"
For Badiou love is more than anything else,

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:28.430
a dedication to an idea.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:32.870
As he says: “love is above all a construction
that lasts.

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:37.530
Real love is one that triumphs lastingly,
sometimes painfully, over the hurdles erected

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.970
by time, space, and the world.”

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:44.539
Sure, Cinderella can get swept off her feet
by her romantic encounter with Prince Charming,

00:06:44.539 --> 00:06:46.300
but what happens next?

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:50.780
We assume they live happily ever after, but
in real life, such ephemeral moments rarely

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:52.300
last “Now what?”

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:53.820
“Happily ever after."

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:55.030
"And after that?"

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:57.160
For Badiou, love is about tenacity.

00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:00.449
It’s about making the encounter endure.

00:07:00.449 --> 00:07:04.229
Consider the encounter that started Beth and
Jerry’s relationship- Beth accidentally

00:07:04.229 --> 00:07:05.900
getting pregnant in high school.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:09.530
"All this time you've been thinking 'what
if that loser Jerry hadn't talked me out of

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:10.530
the abortion?'

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:14.409
Well now you know" In Badiouian terms, this
would be the “event.”

00:07:14.409 --> 00:07:18.610
And yes, this “event” probably could have
been prevented by proper sexual health education,

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:22.460
rendering it nowhere near as romantic as your
average Disney fair, but that’s of little

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.099
consequence to love.

00:07:24.099 --> 00:07:29.259
What matters is continued fidelity to that
event- no matter how banal it may be; to continue

00:07:29.259 --> 00:07:33.999
to say yes in developing the connection, to
perpetuate the relationship in the face of

00:07:33.999 --> 00:07:37.819
pain and hardship- that’s where true love
is forged.

00:07:37.819 --> 00:07:42.050
For Beth this means ignoring the fact that
Jerry can’t hold down a job, or that he’s

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:46.520
a total coward, or that he’s pretty much
the most superfluous man in the universe,

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:51.439
and continue to work through it, to affirm
the thing that brought them together and build

00:07:51.439 --> 00:07:52.790
off the initial event.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:57.749
Per Badiou: “an apparently insignificant
act, but one that is a really radical event

00:07:57.749 --> 00:08:04.039
in life at a micro level, bears universal
meaning in the way it persists and endures.”

00:08:04.039 --> 00:08:08.130
This is the perspective Beth is coming from
when trying to get Summer off the app.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:13.199
All of Summer’s encounters evoke your stereotypical
fairytale connection "You had me at my own

00:08:13.199 --> 00:08:14.199
name."

00:08:14.199 --> 00:08:15.199
“You had me at hello.”

00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:20.189
But in the end, Beth asserts the supremacy
of Badiou-ian love over the monogatron version

00:08:20.189 --> 00:08:24.820
when she checks Lovefinderz to see that it’s
paired her with Boomer dreamboat Ted Danson.

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:29.909
However, instead of pursuing him in all his
silver foxiness, she deletes the app and deepens

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:31.650
her embrace of Jerry.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:35.900
Love isn’t about picking the most viable
candidate out of a dating pool of millions.

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:38.760
It’s about making the choice to endure.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:43.030
To quote Badiou again: “Strictly speaking,
love isn’t a possibility, but rather the

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:46.530
overcoming of something that might appear
to be impossible."

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:49.960
Over the last three seasons we’ve seen Beth
and Jerry struggle through a marriage that

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:55.950
at many times has seemed impossible "You both
hate yourselves, and each other!" and yet,

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:59.270
somehow they’ve overcome their challenges
and stayed together.

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:03.790
And after their separation during season 3,
Beth now knows, more than ever, that real

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:06.260
love is born out of tenacity.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:11.550
At the episode’s close, the supposed “superior
intimacy” of the monogatrons is proven false.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:15.700
Earlier, the leader and his partner seemed
like true soul mates, even enjoying the same

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:18.970
podcast "I found a new podcast we can listen
to tonight.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:19.970
"Love it".

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:23.770
But, as it turns out, that’s not quite enough
"-and I want a divorce!"

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:27.970
"You can have one for a glass of goddamn water!"

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:29.280
But what do you guys think?

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:32.380
Is love a matter of finding the hottest needle
in the haystack?

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:35.230
Is it about passion and mutual self fulfillment?

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:40.380
Or is it about dedication and hard work in
the face of seemingly insurmountable challenges.

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:44.130
And is this any indication that the show may
be getting more hopeful?

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:47.880
Oh- and if you want to hear us muse about
the existential implications of being a shy

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:51.020
pooper, be sure to check out our Rick and
Morty podcast The Squanch.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:54.600
We’re breaking down every episode of the
season, so be sure to subscribe on google

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:58.760
play, stitcher, apple podcasts, and our youtube
channel wisecasts.

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:10.940
And

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:12.400
as always, thanks for watching guys.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:12.940
Peace.

